(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m3-v21"]={"id":"v21","label":"Version 21 · natural teaching notes with following slides","revision":"ling406-modern-m3-v21-2","scripts":{"m3-reading1-p001":[],"m3-reading1-p002":[{"text":"The first thing to remember about **phonemes** is that **phonemes are not letters**. A phoneme is a category of speech sound, while a letter is a written symbol, so the two do not have to match one to one.","children":[]},{"text":"A single letter can take part in different pronunciations, and a single phoneme can be spelled in different ways.","children":["So identifying the letters in a word is a different task from identifying its phonemes, because one concerns writing and the other concerns sound."]}],"m3-reading1-p003":[{"text":"A **mispronouncing contest**, in which people willfully mispronounced words like COLONEL and PARADIGM, made it clear that phonemes are not letters and that **letters are not phonemes** either.","children":["Following the usual letter-to-sound patterns gives a plausible pronunciation that is wrong for the real word."]},{"text":"Scripts differ in how regularly their spelling matches sound, and this difference is called **orthographic depth**.","children":["Pinyin for Mandarin and French are relatively regular scripts."]},{"text":"**English is a relatively deep script** because of its irregularities, while **Finnish and Korean are very shallow scripts**.","children":["A deep script is less predictable than a shallow one, but it is not completely random."]}],"m3-reading1-p004":[{"text":"**Finnish** is a very **transparent** writing system, which is called **shallow** in the terms of the **Orthographic Depth Hypothesis**.","children":["After about a minute of following a beginning Finnish lesson along with its text, the pattern becomes easy to grasp."]},{"text":"Each letter corresponds more or less unambiguously to one phoneme, and **doubled letters** stand for **geminated consonants** (consonants held longer) or **long vowels**.","children":[]},{"text":"Because spelling matches sound so closely, **Finnish children learn to decode very quickly**.","children":["**Decoding** means producing a pronunciation from a written form, so a child who learns the letter-sound matches can read many new words."]}],"m3-reading1-p005":[{"text":"**English is an outlier** among languages that use the Roman alphabet, and one result is that **children learn to decode more slowly in English**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Aro and Wimmer** compared first-grade children learning English with children learning French, German, Dutch, Spanish, Swedish and Finnish.","children":["The children read **pseudowords** aloud, which are made-up words that follow the spelling and sound patterns of their language."]},{"text":"Children learning the other languages produced plausible pronunciations **at a much higher rate** than English learners.","children":["A pseudoword cannot be remembered as a stored word, so success depends on decoding, and the less predictable English spelling makes decoding harder to learn."]}],"m3-reading1-p006":[{"text":"**Korean** writing is even more regular than Finnish, and in Hangul the **symbols for similar phonemes look similar** to one another.","children":[]},{"text":"The letters are arranged into **syllable blocks** with an initial, a medial and sometimes a final position.","children":[]},{"text":"The feature that roughly matches **velar place of articulation** (sounds made with the back of the tongue at the soft palate) is the basic angular **ㄱ shape**.","children":["The velar consonants g, kk and k are written ㄱ, ㄲ and ㅋ, so they share one shape family, with doubling or an extra stroke telling the related sounds apart."]}],"m3-reading1-p007":[{"text":"Pseudowords such as GLORP, SPLING, JONK, FEESH and QUISP can be read aloud even though no reader has them stored as words.","children":[]},{"text":"A reader builds a plausible pronunciation from familiar **English spelling patterns**, so reading them is **decoding in action**.","children":["Success cannot come from retrieving a stored pronunciation, because each item is new, so the reader is using knowledge that **generalizes across words**."]},{"text":"These items test decoding rather than vocabulary or word meaning.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p008":[{"text":"**Lupker, Brown and Colombo** had people read real words and **pseudowords**, here called **nonwords**, aloud and compared how fast they began naming each kind.","children":["Pseudowords were read **nearly as quickly and accurately as real words**, and naming them was **only slightly slower** than naming **low-frequency words**."]},{"text":"The small difference suggests that people decode **fairly naturally, automatically and implicitly**, so an unfamiliar spelling does not force a slow, deliberate effort.","children":[]},{"text":"Computational models of word reading ask how decoding should be characterized.","children":["The central question is whether decoding is best captured by **categorical rules** or by **probabilistic constraints**."]}],"m3-reading1-p009":[{"text":"Pseudowords such as VONTH, PHANGE, STREAD and SOUGH involve **decoding with ambiguity**, because their spellings support **more than one plausible pronunciation** in English.","children":[]},{"text":"A reader's answer depends on the spelling-to-sound patterns learned from earlier words, and different familiar words can favor different answers to the same new form.","children":["These items probe how readers settle uncertainty in spelling-to-sound mappings, so there is no single correct pronunciation that every reader must agree on."]}],"m3-reading1-p010":[{"text":"**Generalization** to new items is a good way to test what kinds of **regularities** people have learned about a system, because a new item cannot have been memorized.","children":["The **Wug Test** is another example of this kind of generalization test."]},{"text":"For STREAD, both /strɛd/ and /strid/ feel acceptable, and it is hard to imagine a **rule set** that covers that intuition.","children":["A categorical rule would pick one output, while a **statistical account** can give different amounts of support to each pronunciation."]},{"text":"So a model of reading has to explain why both answers seem fine, not just produce one of them.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p011":[{"text":"It is uncontroversial that some **decoding** is part of normal reading, and that **irregular** or **inconsistent** words are **harder to recognize** than regular words, which is the **regularity effect**.","children":[]},{"text":"The effect is straightforward for words like PINT, ACHE and CHOIR, because the usual spelling-to-sound patterns would give the wrong pronunciation.","children":[]},{"text":"It is less clear-cut for **neighborhoods**, which are sets of words sharing a spelling pattern, such as ROLL, POLL, TOLL and DOLL or COOK, TOOK, BOOK and SPOOK.","children":["In each set most members share one pronunciation while DOLL or SPOOK differs, so the question becomes how much support a pronunciation gets from similar words."]}],"m3-reading1-p012":[{"text":"There is a lot of evidence that readers use **sublexical information**, meaning units smaller than a whole word, especially when turning spelling into sound.","children":["People read nonwords quickly and accurately, and words that follow typical spelling-to-sound patterns are easier to read than words that do not."]},{"text":"That leaves the question of **lexical processes**, which use knowledge of **particular words**.","children":["Nonwords test generalization, while differences among familiar words can reveal what readers know about specific words, so a full account needs both kinds of information."]}],"m3-reading1-p013":[{"text":"The **Interactive Activation Model** of **McClelland and Rumelhart** links visual **features**, **letters** and **words**, so features activate letters and letters activate candidate words such as WORK, WORD, WEAR and WEAK.","children":[]},{"text":"When the last letter of WORK is partly covered, knowledge of the word helps identify it.","children":["Active word candidates send **feedback** to their letters, so K gains activation over time while R rises a little and fades and D stays low."]},{"text":"So letter recognition can depend on the surrounding word, and the network settles on an answer gradually rather than all at once.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p014":[{"text":"In the **Interactive Activation Model**, **feedback** from active **known words** increases the activation of the **letters that occur in those words**.","children":[]},{"text":"This makes recognition **interactive**, because information does not only flow upward from features to a finished word.","children":["Letter-to-word activation gives evidence for a word candidate, and word-to-letter feedback, called **top-down feedback**, supports that candidate's letters."]},{"text":"So an **ambiguous letter** becomes easier to recognize when it appears inside a familiar word.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p015":[{"text":"Two **lexical effects** have already come up, the overall effect of **lexicality** and the effect of **word frequency**.","children":["Lexicality is whether an item is a real word, and real words are named faster than nonwords."]},{"text":"**Word frequency** is how often a word has been encountered, and just as in **picture naming**, more past encounters with a word lead to **faster naming**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Balota and colleagues** found that this effect is **robust over a very large number of observations**, using data from the **English Lexicon Project**.","children":["Naming time drops steadily as frequency rises across a very large set of words, so the effect does not depend on a few easy examples."]}],"m3-reading1-p016":[{"text":"One worry about **frequency effects** was that they might be **confounds** of word length and other variables, meaning that frequent words might be fast only because they are shorter.","children":[]},{"text":"A **multiple regression analysis** of about three thousand words read by thirty young and older adults tested this by asking whether frequency still predicts response time once the other word properties are taken into account.","children":["**Objective and subjective frequency** both still predicted response time in **lexical decision** (deciding whether a letter string is a real word) and **naming** for both age groups.","**Higher frequency** went with **faster responses**, so the frequency effect **is not just a confound** of length."]}],"m3-reading1-p017":[{"text":"The **frequency by regularity interaction** is a landmark finding in psycholinguistics, and it has shaped other areas, most immediately **inflectional morphology**.","children":[]},{"text":"The **frequency effect is much larger for exception words than for regular words**.","children":["Among **high-frequency** words, exception and regular words are named about equally fast, but among **low-frequency** words, exception words are named much more slowly."]},{"text":"An **interaction** means the effect of one factor depends on the other, so regularity costs a lot for rare words and very little for common ones.","children":["One interpretation is that common words are read through word knowledge so quickly that decoding barely matters."]}],"m3-reading1-p018":[{"text":"One interpretation of the interaction is that word recognition uses **two separate processes**.","children":["The **sublexical process** is decoding, and the **lexical process** is knowledge of a particular word, including its learned pronunciation."]},{"text":"For **high-frequency words**, the lexical process is so fast and efficient that the sublexical process has **little influence**.","children":[]},{"text":"The sublexical process only shows up when people read **nonwords** or **low-frequency words**.","children":["Nonwords have no stored word to retrieve, and for a rare exception word the slower lexical process gives the regularized decoding output time to interfere."]}],"m3-reading1-p019":[{"text":"**Acquired phonological dyslexia** is reading difficulty caused by **brain injury**, which is why it is called acquired rather than **developmental dyslexia**.","children":["These patients have difficulty **specifically with pseudoword naming**."]},{"text":"**Cacciopo van Vliet and colleagues** compared real-word reading with several nonword reading tests in two patients, MO and IB.","children":["Both patients read real words **almost perfectly** across concreteness, word class and frequency, but gave plausible pronunciations for **far fewer nonwords**."]},{"text":"So familiar-word reading can stay intact while **decoding new letter strings** is badly damaged, although the deficit is relative because some nonwords were still read well.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p020":[{"text":"**Acquired surface dyslexia** is also reading difficulty that follows **brain injury**, but here the difficulty is **specifically with irregular words**.","children":[]},{"text":"The mispronunciations tend to be **regularizations**, meaning the word is read with the common spelling-to-sound pattern instead of its learned pronunciation.","children":["DEAF is read as “deef”, SHOE as “show”, ONE as “own” and STEAK as “steek”."]},{"text":"The patients MP and KT read regular words and nonwords well and did worst on **low-frequency exception words**.","children":["Most of their errors on exceptions were regularizations, so the errors are systematic rather than random failures to see the letters."]}],"m3-reading1-p021":[{"text":"Patients with **acquired surface dyslexia** struggle with **irregular words**, make **regularization** errors and have **relatively spared pseudoword reading**.","children":[]},{"text":"Patients with **acquired phonological dyslexia** struggle with **pseudoword naming** but have **relatively spared exception word reading**.","children":[]},{"text":"These opposite patterns form a classic **double dissociation**, in which one group is impaired on one task but not the other while another group shows the reverse.","children":["Relatively spared does not mean perfect, because the evidence lies in the contrast between the two patterns of performance."]}],"m3-reading1-p022":[{"text":"**Double dissociations** are so powerful because they are hard to explain by saying that one task is **simply harder** than the other.","children":["If pseudowords were just harder than exception words, brain damage should keep hurting pseudoword reading more, and no patient would show the reverse pattern."]},{"text":"A reversal across patients suggests that the two tasks rely on **separable processes**, so pseudowords and exception words place different demands on the reading system.","children":["The evidence still needs a theory of what those processes are, and two low scores without an opposite pattern are not a double dissociation."]}],"m3-reading1-p023":[{"text":"Double dissociations give evidence that the processes behind **pseudoword reading** and **irregular word reading** are **separable**.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Morton's logogen model**, **phonological dyslexia** can come from specific damage to the **grapheme-phoneme conversion** module, which turns letters into sounds.","children":["Pseudowords suffer because they have no stored word pronunciation to fall back on."]},{"text":"**Surface dyslexia** can come from damage to the input or output **logogens**, which are stored word representations, or to the connections between them.","children":["Exception words then depend on conversion, which gives the wrong, regularized pronunciation, and these boxes are parts of a theory rather than a map of brain regions."]}],"m3-reading1-p024":[{"text":"**Patterson** gave early signs that the dual-route account is **not so simple**.","children":[]},{"text":"The dual-route view held that surface dyslexia errors come from the **non-lexical** grapheme-phoneme route, so they should show **no lexical influences**.","children":["Yet **Marcel** looked carefully at the errors and found lexical influences, and **neologisms**, which are made-up nonword responses, were rarer than a purely non-lexical process predicts.","A patient read “incense” as “increase”, a real word, when a purely letter-to-sound error should have produced the neologism “inkense”."]},{"text":"Patterson saw two options, either surface dyslexia reflects a **more complicated combination of the two routes** or non-lexical reading is itself a **more complicated route**.","children":["Patterson suggested that combining normal and patient evidence holds the best promise for progress."]}],"m3-reading2-p001":[],"m3-reading2-p002":[{"text":"**Dual-route models** of visual word recognition propose two reading routes because several reading findings seem to need two separate processes. **Pseudoword reading** (reading made-up letter strings) is fast and automatic, real **word reading** is faster, and **high-frequency words** are read fastest.","children":["Accuracy and response time also depend on **regularity**, and **frequency and regularity interact**.","In **acquired dyslexia**, pseudoword reading and irregular word reading can be impaired separately, so the two abilities are **dissociable**."]},{"text":"The models therefore hypothesize a **sublexical route** that turns spelling into sound through **grapheme-to-phoneme conversion**, also called **decoding**, and a **lexical route** that recognizes real words.","children":["A **grapheme** is a written unit that is linked to a sound."]}],"m3-reading2-p003":[{"text":"**Coltheart and colleagues** built the **Dual Route Cascaded model**, or **DRC**, which joins an interactive lexical route to a separate rule route. Its lexical portion is just the **Interactive Activation model**, with an **orthographic lexicon** for written word forms and a **phonological lexicon** for spoken word forms.","children":[]},{"text":"The DRC has **no implementation of semantics**, because both lexicons are independent of meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"The **Grapheme-Phoneme Rule System** runs on completely different processing assumptions from the interactive portion, so rule-based conversion and word-form activation are separate mechanisms.","children":["The rule path joins the output system instead of becoming another interactive layer of the lexicon."]}],"m3-reading2-p004":[{"text":"The **GPC rules** (the grapheme-phoneme conversion rules) are **activated serially**, letter by letter, and they are **not affected by word frequency** in any way. They also receive **no feedback**, so nothing in the lexicon changes how a rule applies.","children":[]},{"text":"The GPC route does **not interact with the lexical route** except in the **phoneme response buffer**, where both routes connect to the same output units.","children":["Because they share those units, the two routes can **compete** to activate different pronunciations.","For an exception word, the lexical route can push the correct pronunciation while the rules push a **regularized** one."]}],"m3-reading2-p005":[{"text":"The GPC rules map written units onto phonemes, and the full rule list includes **multiletter rules** such as eigh and ough. It also includes **context-sensitive rules** that apply only next to certain letters, such as c before e or i.","children":["Each rule also states whether it applies at the beginning, middle, end or all positions of a word."]},{"text":"A rule list alone is not enough, because several rules can match the same letters. The model also needs **meta-rules** that decide when to apply rules at different levels of analysis.","children":["For instance, **larger-unit rules supersede smaller-unit rules**, so a multiletter rule wins over the rules for its single letters."]}],"m3-reading2-p006":[{"text":"The GPC rules are **not totally independent of statistics**, because they are often based on the **most frequent pronunciation** for a letter or combination of letters.","children":[]},{"text":"Once chosen, though, the rules are **fixed and categorical**, so every rule behaves the same way during processing no matter how strong its support was.","children":["EIGH is pronounced /eI/ about two thirds of the time and /aI/ the rest, and the separate EIGH rule for spellings without a final T always gives /eI/.","OUGH is spread over five pronunciations, so its rule has **weaker statistical support** than the EIGH rule, yet both work the same way as rules."]}],"m3-reading2-p007":[{"text":"The DRC's rules are **kind of silly**, in the sense that they **miss a lot of cases**, but this was seen as a **feature, not a bug**. Better rules could be written by taking statistics into account more flexibly, and the DRC's rules did go through several revisions to improve its **nonword reading**.","children":[]},{"text":"The DRC argues that people **do not use finely tuned rules** for the GPC route. If they did, there would be no **regularization errors** in **surface dyslexia** and no **slow-down for low-frequency exception words**.","children":["So the simple rules are meant to reproduce the errors people really make, not to pronounce as many words correctly as possible."]}],"m3-reading2-p008":[{"text":"The DRC borrows the idea of **informational encapsulation**, in which a process does not use every source of relevant knowledge, from models of syntax. There, parsing strategies such as **late closure** and **minimal attachment** were motivated by syntactic complexity only, so parsing was kept separate from world knowledge and lexical co-occurrence statistics.","children":[]},{"text":"The dual-route models are designed to be **similarly modular**, so the GPC route chugs along without consulting word knowledge. It produces correct pronunciations for many words and **incorrect responses to some exceptions**.","children":["Its operation becomes visible when people make **regularization errors** and when people are made to **name pseudowords**, because a pseudoword has no stored entry for the lexical route to supply."]}],"m3-reading2-p009":[{"text":"The DRC can **simulate a lot of phenomena** in visual word recognition, across **reading aloud**, **lexical decision** and the **acquired dyslexias**.","children":["Reading aloud simulations include frequency, lexicality, regularity and consistency effects and the interaction of regularity with frequency.","Acquired dyslexia simulations include exception, regular and nonword reading in surface dyslexia and pseudohomophone effects in phonological dyslexia."]},{"text":"**Coltheart and colleagues** built the architecture from a quarter century of reading research rather than from backpropagation, because they held that **human behavioral data** are the major source of constraint on such models.","children":["Covering many effects with one architecture is strong support, but it also matters which assumptions change between tasks."]}],"m3-reading2-p010":[{"text":"Modeling **lexical decision**, which is deciding whether a letter string is a word, sometimes means going to **extremes to make contact with data**. The DRC first **reduces letter-word inhibition**, a change that makes the task harder but without which the results make no sense.","children":[]},{"text":"The model answers **yes** if any single word passes an activity threshold, or if **total activity across the orthographic input lexicon** passes a second threshold that drops when the lexicon is active early.","children":["It answers **no** if neither criterion is met by a **deadline** counted in processing cycles, and that deadline is **nearly twice as fast** as the model normally reads aloud.","These thresholds and deadlines are extra decision assumptions added on top of the basic two-route model."]}],"m3-reading2-p011":[{"text":"The **key feature of the DRC is modularity**, and the GPC route and lexical route are modular in three ways. Each route has its **own processing assumptions**, and the two routes are **informationally encapsulated** from one another, so neither uses the information inside the other.","children":[]},{"text":"The routes are also **domain-specific**, which means each one accepts only its own kind of input.","children":["The GPC rules can take only **letters as inputs**, and they can pass **phoneme activations** only to the phoneme system."]}],"m3-reading2-p012":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland** took an **emergentist approach** with a **PDP model** of visual word recognition, a network that learns and stores knowledge spread across its connections. They treated **reading skill** as emerging from **mappings among spelling, pronunciation and meaning**, which are **orthography**, **phonology** and **semantics**.","children":[]},{"text":"They modeled how these mappings are learned by training a **simple associative network**.","children":["They studied only the mapping from **orthography to phonology**, called **O to P**, in a **single route** used for every kind of word."]},{"text":"So reading skill comes from learning, rather than from a hand-built list of rules and word entries.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p013":[{"text":"The PDP model of **Seidenberg and McClelland** makes **different assumptions** from dual-route models. **Rules** are just **generalizations from lots of words**, not a separate process, and the **lexicon is distributed**, so some whole-word information is stored in the O to P mappings and some in other parts of the system.","children":["The model still holds knowledge of individual words, but that knowledge is spread across the network."]},{"text":"It also follows a **different research strategy**, because it explains reading through **domain-general learning mechanisms** instead of **reverse-engineering** the system from adult and neuropsychological data.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p014":[{"text":"The PDP model captured the **frequency by regularity interaction** without assuming that frequency effects and regularity effects come from **different processes**.","children":[]},{"text":"This works because **regular words** are consistent with statistical patterns from many other words, while **exception words** are inconsistent with those patterns.","children":["Frequency also affects **how well each word is learned**, so **highly frequent words** are less affected by their inconsistency with other words.","A low-frequency exception gets weak learning of its own and no help from the shared patterns, so it is the hardest case to read."]}],"m3-reading2-p015":[{"text":"The PDP account redefines **regularity** in terms of **consistency**, which depends on a word's **friends**, similar spellings with the same pronunciation, and its **enemies**, similar spellings with a competing pronunciation.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regular consistent** words such as LATE and DUST have many friends and no enemies. **Regular inconsistent** words such as GAVE and MINT have many friends but at least one enemy, such as HAVE for GAVE and PINT for MINT.","children":["**Ambiguous** words such as DOWN and KNOWN, or POUR and SOUR, have about equal numbers of friends and enemies.","**Exception** words such as HAVE and PINT have few if any friends and many enemies."]}],"m3-reading2-p016":[{"text":"In principle, a PDP model reads **words and nonwords in the same route**. **Nonwords** are named through the **same set of weights** used for words, so no separate rule module and no stored entry for the nonword is needed.","children":[]},{"text":"**Generalization** to a new spelling takes advantage of the **same statistical patterns** that give rise to **regularity effects**.","children":["A new spelling overlaps with familiar spellings, so the learned spelling-to-sound patterns carry over to it."]}],"m3-reading2-p017":[{"text":"In practice, the early PDP model was **not so great**, because its **nonword accuracy was not very good**. This is a **classic case of ambiguity in model evaluation**, because a failed simulation does not say what caused the failure.","children":[]},{"text":"The failure could mean the **theoretical claim** was wrong, the claim that nonwords and exceptions can be read by the **same associative mechanism**.","children":["Or the problem could be **specific to the way the model was made**."]},{"text":"Later work pointed to the second answer, because the model's **phonological representations** were poor and better ones improved nonword reading.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p018":[{"text":"Many studies find a **relationship between decoding**, meaning nonword reading, **and phonological processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"The phonological representations in the model of **Seidenberg and McClelland** were sort of weird, because they used **Wickelfeatures**.","children":["A model by **Plaut and colleagues** **improved the phonological representations** and produced **better performance on nonwords**."]},{"text":"So at least part of the earlier failure came from how the model was made, and decoding improved without adding a rule system.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p019":[{"text":"Phonological representations matter for **nonword generalization** because **generalization depends on similarity**, a principle inherited from **behaviorism**. **Poor phonological representations**, such as Wickelfeatures, fail to capture the **similarity space** of words in the language, so learning cannot transfer well to new forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Word-specific information** is **more robust** to poor representations, because a familiar word does not depend entirely on generalization.","children":["A nonword has no training history of its own, so it suffers most when similarity is captured badly."]}],"m3-reading2-p020":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg** tested the link between phonology and nonword generalization more systematically by **manipulating the amount of noise** in the model's phonological representations. More noise made the sound representations less reliable.","children":[]},{"text":"The simulations **replicated key findings on developmental dyslexia**.","children":["The **degree of phonological deficit** was related to the degree of **phonological dyslexia**, which is poor nonword decoding.","**Word recognition was relatively spared**, because familiar words lean less on generalization."]},{"text":"So a selective reading deficit can emerge from degraded phonology, without any separate nonword mechanism.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p021":[{"text":"The PDP account also has to explain the **double dissociations** in acquired dyslexia. Nonword reading can already be **specifically impaired** in these models, even though they have **no nonword reading mechanism**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues** explored the other end of the dissociation, **surface dyslexia**. They implemented a **simplified semantic system** and then **lesioned** it, meaning they damaged that part of the model.","children":["The lesions to semantics produced a **specific impairment on exception words**, because exceptions get little support from spelling-sound patterns and lean on meaning."]}],"m3-reading2-p022":[{"text":"The triangle model is **not just the dual-route model renamed**. **Semantic representations are directly involved**, whereas the DRC is committed to a lexicon independent of semantics.","children":["A later model by **Harm and Seidenberg** implemented featural semantics and simulated the mapping of spelling to meaning."]},{"text":"The **O to P** and **S to P** mappings (spelling to sound and meaning to sound) share the **same processing assumptions and learning algorithm**, and both are **probabilistic**.","children":[]},{"text":"The pathways **interact over the course of learning**, so the O to P pathway develops differently alongside an O to S to P pathway.","children":["This **interactivity and gradedness** let the models capture that double dissociations are **never entirely clean**. Phonological dyslexics are not perfect on exception words, and surface dyslexics are not perfect on nonwords."]}],"m3-reading2-p023":[{"text":"**Woollams and colleagues** tested the model's prediction of a **strong association** between **semantic abilities** and **exception word reading**. They compared simulations that varied the strength of semantic activation with **semantic dementia** patients who varied in a composite semantic score.","children":[]},{"text":"In both the models and the patients, **weaker semantics went with poorer low-frequency exception reading**.","children":["The relation was tighter in the models than in the patients, whose scores were more spread out."]},{"text":"So exception word reading tracks semantic ability, as the triangle account predicts.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p024":[{"text":"The association still **misses a few data points**. A few patients are **statistical outliers**, because their low-frequency exception reading stays very high, in some cases despite reduced semantic scores.","children":[]},{"text":"**Woollams and colleagues** would argue that these points should be treated the way **outliers are normally treated** when evaluating the fit of a model to data.","children":["A graded association does not predict zero variability, so a few unusual patients do not by themselves overturn it."]}],"m3-reading2-p025":[{"text":"Because **semantic dementia** is a **progressive condition**, Woollams and colleagues could follow how exception word reading changed **as semantic abilities declined**.","children":[]},{"text":"**In most cases**, when a patient's semantic abilities declined over time, **her reading ability also declined**.","children":["Patients with multiple observations also showed **regression to the mean**, moving back toward the overall relation on retesting.","If the dissociation between semantics and exception word reading came from **idiosyncratic outliers**, it would show just this pattern, so the unusual scores look like noise rather than a separate pattern."]}],"m3-reading2-p026":[{"text":"**Coltheart and colleagues** simulated the same surface dyslexia data with the DRC. They generated a population of **forty thousand model patients** varying on two parameters, the **integrity of the lexical route** and the **integrity of the GPC rules**.","children":[]},{"text":"For each patient in the Woollams data, they **selected the model run that best fit** that patient's data.","children":["In this way they produced an **excellent fit to the data**, but the fit came from choosing among many possible settings after seeing each patient."]}],"m3-reading2-p027":[{"text":"Someone could argue that this **better fit is actually a bad thing** because it relies on **much greater flexibility**. Picking the best match from forty thousand model patients can reproduce each patient without the model **predicting** the relation beforehand.","children":[]},{"text":"The triangle model predicted the link between semantics and exception reading from its own assumptions.","children":["So **goodness of fit** is only one part of evaluating a model, and it matters how much freedom produced the fit."]}],"m3-reading2-p028":[{"text":"**Dual-route** and **triangle models** both cover a range of reading data, including some basic facts. **Decoding is fast and automatic**, as in pseudoword reading, and words that **diverge from typical spelling-to-sound mappings** are harder to read.","children":["This **regularity** or **consistency effect** interacts with **word frequency**.","**Pseudoword reading** and **exception or inconsistent word reading** are dissociable in neuropsychological patients."]},{"text":"The models agree on these findings but differ on the mechanisms that produce them.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p029":[{"text":"The two models give different explanations for each phenomenon. For **pseudoword reading and regularity effects**, the dual-route model uses a dedicated **GPC rule system**, while the triangle model uses **statistical regularities** learned over many words.","children":["For **frequency effects**, the dual-route model uses **distinct input and output lexicons** separate from the GPC rules, while in the triangle model **more exposure to a word** gives better learning for that word."]},{"text":"For **phonological dyslexia**, the dual-route model points to damage in the **GPC route**, while the triangle model points to damage in **phonological processing generally**.","children":["For **surface dyslexia**, the dual-route model points to damage in the **lexical route**, while the triangle model points to damage in **semantic processes generally**."]}],"m3-reading2-p030":[{"text":"The **key contrast** is dissociations against associations. **Dual-route models** are generally motivated by **double dissociations**, so because pseudowords and exception words can be impaired independently, they are modeled as **independent processes**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Statistical learning models** instead reveal **associations** between reading and broader language abilities.","children":["**Phonology** is important for learning and using O to P generalizations, which links it to phonological dyslexia.","**Semantics** is important for words where O to P generalization is **ambiguous**, which links it to surface dyslexia."]}],"m3-reading2-p031":[],"m3-morphology-p001":[],"m3-morphology-p002":[{"text":"**Morphology**, the way words are built from meaningful parts, is **generative**, because English speakers can form new words they never memorized one by one.","children":[]},{"text":"At one point there was no noun \"generative,\" just the verb \"to generate,\" but English has a process for turning verbs into nouns, and that process is **derivational morphology**.","children":["**Inflectional morphology** adds grammatical information to an existing word, and in English it pretty much means the **plural** and the **past tense**."]},{"text":"Extending a pattern can even produce playful forms, as in \"She found me crying and she crew too and we both crode.\"","children":["Crew and crode are not standard past tenses of cry, but they come from extending irregular-sounding patterns to a new verb, so they reveal **productive use of patterns** rather than recall of forms someone heard."]}],"m3-morphology-p003":[{"text":"**Jean Berko Gleason** ran the classic **wug test**, which measures how readily people extend an inflection to a word they have never heard.","children":[]},{"text":"A child sees a drawing of one creature labeled \"This is a WUG,\" then sees two of them and has to finish the sentence \"There are two\" with the right word.","children":["Saying **wugs** means the child applied plural knowledge to a brand-new noun, and because the word is new, the answer cannot be a memorized form."]},{"text":"The study reveals how readily people generalize the **past tense** to new words, and children do this too, which is why the test uses cute drawings.","children":["Success reveals **productive knowledge**, which is the ability to apply a learned pattern to new items."]}],"m3-morphology-p004":[{"text":"The English **past tense** has **regular** forms, which add -ed, and **irregular** forms, which change in their own way.","children":[]},{"text":"Regular verbs include show and showed, bake and baked, and wade and waded, and new verbs get the same ending, so blick becomes blicked, trog becomes trogged and wumble becomes wumbled.","children":["Because those nonwords were never heard before, they test whether a speaker can **extend** the regular pattern."]},{"text":"Irregular verbs include go and went, take and took, eat and ate, and sleep and slept.","children":["Any theory of morphology has to explain both these **exceptions** and the **productive extension** of the pattern to novel verbs."]}],"m3-morphology-p005":[{"text":"**Pinker and colleagues** proposed the **Words and Rules** account, in which words and rules are **separate processes** with separate representational formats.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regular** past tenses come from a **symbolic rule** such as \"add -ed,\" so the rule can apply to any new verb without a stored entry for its past tense.","children":[]},{"text":"**Irregular** past tenses are **lexicalized**, meaning stored as individual words, inside an **associative network** that links each verb to its own past form.","children":["So remembered exceptions and productive rule use count as two different kinds of knowledge, not as one system."]}],"m3-morphology-p006":[{"text":"**Pinker and Ullman** proposed a **dual route** model of the past tense, in which a word stem such as walk or hold, plus a grammatical feature such as past tense, feeds two routes.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexicon** route uses lookup and association in **declarative memory** to retrieve an irregular such as held, and it is tied to temporo-parietal cortex.","children":["The **grammar** route uses combination in the **procedural system** to build walk plus -ed, and it is tied to frontal cortex and basal ganglia, while a **blocking** link lets a retrieved irregular stop the regular form."]},{"text":"It is similar to the dual-route model of reading because both split **word-specific knowledge** from a **productive general operation**, but it differs because reading converts spelling into sound, while this model changes a stem to mark a grammatical feature.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p007":[{"text":"The past-tense model and the dual-route model of reading have a **similar architecture** but **different origins**.","children":[]},{"text":"The dual-route model of reading was developed from **neuropsychological double dissociations**, in which one patient loses one ability while another patient loses the opposite one.","children":["Pinker and colleagues were motivated by other concerns."]},{"text":"**Generative linguistics** of the time was mostly about **categorical, symbolic, rule-based grammars**, and these rules often have **exceptions**.","children":["So the Words and Rules model came from an argument about keeping grammar rules apart from exceptions, rather than from patient dissociations."]}],"m3-morphology-p008":[{"text":"Rather than make the grammar **inelegant** by adding machinery to handle exceptions, linguists would often just leave the exceptions out of the grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"The **dual-process model** offers a way to handle the exceptions while keeping the grammar elegant, symbolic and categorical.","children":["Its second process has all the properties that the rule route does not have."]},{"text":"That process is **word-specific**, or lexical, it is **sensitive to frequency of occurrence**, and it is **sensitive to phonological similarity**, as in keep and kept, weep and wept, and leap and leapt.","children":["Because similar-sounding irregulars share a pattern, an exception can belong to a small family instead of standing completely alone."]}],"m3-morphology-p009":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland** offered an alternative in which the past tense is **all associative learning**, with no separate rule.","children":[]},{"text":"Their network encodes the sound of the **root form** as **Wickelfeatures**, the model's features for sound patterns, and passes them through a **pattern associator** with **modifiable connections** to Wickelfeatures of the past tense, which are decoded back into sounds.","children":["The encoding and decoding networks are fixed, so all of the learning happens in the modifiable connections."]},{"text":"One mechanism learns both **regular** and **irregular** verbs, so regularity is a pattern picked up from many examples rather than a stored rule.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p010":[{"text":"Rumelhart and McClelland built one of the first learning models of a language process, so they focused on capturing **developmental phenomena**, especially **U-shaped learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"Some frequent irregular verbs are learned quite early, so a young child correctly says ran.","children":["Then, spontaneously, the child starts producing **regularizations** such as runned, which apply the regular pattern to an irregular verb.","Eventually the child starts producing the correct form ran again."]},{"text":"Learning is called U-shaped because accuracy goes down before it goes back up, so children often get worse before they get better.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p011":[{"text":"U-shaped learning matters to the **Words and Rules** approach because that approach explains the dip as a series of **distinct stages and processes**.","children":[]},{"text":"Early correct forms are just **imitations**, so they do not depend on the same mechanisms as adult processing.","children":["When the rule is first learned, it is applied to all cases, because the rule is the **best hypothesis** for verbs in general, and this produces runned."]},{"text":"Over time, exceptions are learned **associatively**, one by one, and added to lexical knowledge, while the **blocking process** develops.","children":["Blocking lets the retrieved irregular stop the regular form, so the child returns to saying ran."]}],"m3-morphology-p012":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland's** model also produces U-shaped learning, because its **training set** follows data about the input to children, which changes over time.","children":[]},{"text":"Early on, irregular verbs predominate, because the highest-frequency verbs tend to have irregular past tenses, such as GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO.","children":["As the vocabulary expands to include more regular forms, the model **reorganizes** to capture those regularities."]},{"text":"This reorganization **interferes** with some of the early-learned irregulars, so their correct responses weaken while regularized alternatives briefly grow stronger.","children":["With more training the correct irregular responses recover, and no rule component is ever added."]}],"m3-morphology-p013":[{"text":"The model was trained on a **small number of verbs**, so its makers could run a **wug test with real words** that the model had never seen.","children":[]},{"text":"They gave it **unfamiliar low-frequency regular verbs** such as guard, kid, carp, shape and mail and recorded the past tenses it produced.","children":["A real English verb counts as novel for the model when it was left out of training."]},{"text":"The model generated most **Wickelfeatures** correctly for both regular and irregular verbs, with regulars somewhat higher.","children":["Getting most features right is not the same as producing a whole correct word, and some responses were odd forms such as membled for mail."]}],"m3-morphology-p014":[{"text":"The model also made a few responses that **added or changed consonants**, which people rarely do.","children":[]},{"text":"These were maded as the past tense of mate, squawked for squat, membled for mail, and toureder for tour.","children":["The model's authors judged it unlikely that humans would make these errors, especially the last two, but noted that the responses were mostly **near threshold**."]},{"text":"They suggested that an **auto-associative network** among the output units could **clean up** the output pattern and filter out these **bizarre responses**.","children":["They had not yet implemented this suggestion, so the fix remained an untested proposal."]}],"m3-morphology-p015":[{"text":"**Prince and Pinker** argued that a model built only from features cannot keep competing outputs from **blending** together.","children":[]},{"text":"In Rumelhart and McClelland's model, various subregularities and the regular rule all compete to activate **Wickelfeatures**, so the input sing can activate features for sing, sang, sung, singed and sanged all at once.","children":["The model never puts out any single word, only an unordered collection of features, many of them contradictory."]},{"text":"So it cannot avoid blending distinct competing outputs into **bizarre hybrids**, such as toureder as the past of tour or membled for mail.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p016":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg** built a newer model in which speech input, **semantics** and speech output are linked through learned connections.","children":[]},{"text":"Their premise is that **rule-like generalization** is supported by **phonological representations**, the sound patterns of words.","children":["So regular past tenses and new forms depend mainly on reliable sound knowledge."]},{"text":"**Irregular** forms require **greater support from semantics**, the meaning of the specific word, because their past tense does not follow the common sound pattern.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p017":[{"text":"The **Joanisse and Seidenberg** model targets a **dissociation** between **irregular** and **nonword** past tense in **neuropsychological data**, where some patients do worse on one and other patients do worse on the other.","children":[]},{"text":"A nonword has no stored meaning, so its past tense depends on **phonological generalization**.","children":["A known irregular does not follow the common pattern, so it leans on **semantic** support."]},{"text":"So if damaging phonology produces one patient pattern and damaging semantics produces the other, a single system can explain both sides without separate rule and word routes.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p018":[{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** follows injury to **left anterior** brain regions and brings **poor speech production**.","children":[]},{"text":"These patients have problems with **regular morphology**, especially **nonwords** such as wug and wugged or blick and blicked.","children":["Their nonword accuracy is clearly the lowest, while regulars and irregulars are both much higher, with irregulars slightly ahead."]},{"text":"A nonword has no stored past tense, so the patient must generate it from the sound pattern alone, which makes this condition the hardest.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p019":[{"text":"When **phonology** is damaged in the model, its ability to compute past tense forms is **reduced**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Nonwords** become worse than both regulars and irregulars, and regulars are numerically worse than irregulars.","children":["This profile closely matches the Broca's patients across all three conditions."]},{"text":"So a broad **phonological impairment** can produce what looks like a selective loss of regular morphology, without removing any symbolic rule.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p020":[{"text":"Other patients have **problems with irregulars**, including **Wernicke's** and **Alzheimer's** patients and **anomic** patients with injury to **inferior and middle temporal** brain regions.","children":[]},{"text":"These patients are poor on **irregular** past tenses, while their regular and nonword past tenses stay much higher.","children":["This is the reverse of the Broca's pattern, so together the two groups form the dissociation the model is aiming at.","A theory then has to explain why known irregulars suffer in these patients when new forms do not."]}],"m3-morphology-p021":[{"text":"When **semantics** is damaged in the model, **irregulars** become worse than regulars and nonwords, and nonwords are a bit worse than regulars.","children":[]},{"text":"The same profile appears in **Alzheimer's patients**, whose irregulars are also the lowest of the three types.","children":["Regular and new forms can still rely on the common sound pattern, so losing meaning hurts them less."]},{"text":"So **semantic impairment** produces the reverse of the phonological-damage profile, because irregulars depend more on meaning.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p022":[{"text":"**Bird, Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, McClelland and Seidenberg** found that in **Broca's patients**, the difference between regulars and irregulars is **reduced** when items are adequately **controlled for phonology**.","children":["So part of the apparent regular deficit may come from sound differences between the items rather than from a lost rule."]},{"text":"**Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, Hodges and McClelland** found that in **semantic dementia** patients, the degree of impairment with irregulars **correlates strongly** with the severity of the **semantic deficit**.","children":["This link is what the phonology and semantics account predicts, because irregulars lean on meaning."]},{"text":"Both results raise **other issues with the double dissociation data**, which had been read as evidence for separate rule and word systems.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p023":[{"text":"The past-tense account divides the work between phonology and semantics in the same way as the **triangle model** of dyslexia.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological representations** support **generalization**, which is a result of **statistical learning** about patterns in the mapping from present to past.","children":["So impaired phonological representations damage these generalizations."]},{"text":"**Semantic representations** support **idiosyncratic forms**, the ones that deviate from the overwhelmingly regular \"add -ed\" pattern and need support from meaning.","children":["Some irregular forms can take advantage of **sub-patterns** shared with similar verbs, so they are likely to be **spared**."]}],"m3-morphology-p024":[{"text":"**Specific Language Impairment**, or SLI, is **impaired language development** in people whose nonverbal IQ and performance are in the normal range.","children":[]},{"text":"It affects grammar, including both **morphology** and **syntax**.","children":["It also affects **phonology**, **reading**, **working memory** and **speech perception**, which reach beyond grammar."]},{"text":"Because the problems are this broad, a theory that places the deficit only in grammatical rules has to explain these other weaknesses as well.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p025":[{"text":"**Past tense deficits** are a **sine qua non** of SLI, meaning a feature the disorder is never without.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier claims by **Gopnik, van der Lely, Pinker, Ullman and Clahsen** suggested that the deficit was **specific to rules**.","children":["On this view SLI could be evidence that language acquisition requires **domain-specific mechanisms**, ones used only for language."]},{"text":"A deficit in learning rules predicts **problems with regulars and nonwords, but not irregulars**, because irregulars are stored as words.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p026":[{"text":"**Van der Lely and Ullman** compared past tense accuracy in an **SLI** group and **same-age controls** on regular verbs, irregular verbs and nonwords.","children":[]},{"text":"The SLI group was **low on all three**, with regulars slightly above irregulars and nonwords the lowest, while the controls scored far higher on every type.","children":["So irregulars were impaired too, rather than spared as a pure rule deficit predicts."]},{"text":"The result is a **broad deficit** with nonwords hardest, and an account has to explain that whole pattern.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p027":[{"text":"A **developmental model** with **weakened phonology** learned past tenses less well across all three item types.","children":[]},{"text":"With a **severe phonological deficit** it was very poor, and with a **weaker phonological deficit** it did better, but in both cases regulars were highest, irregulars next and **nonwords lowest**.","children":["The **intact** model did much better, both at the end of training and at an earlier point in training."]},{"text":"So a phonological learning weakness reproduces the main SLI pattern, with no missing grammatical rule.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p028":[{"text":"In the model, **phonology** affects the development of **all forms**, because every past tense, whether regular, irregular or new, has to be produced as sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"So weakening phonology lowers performance on regulars, irregulars and nonwords alike.","children":["Phonology is especially important to **nonwords**, because a new word has no item-specific learning and depends entirely on generalizing the sound pattern."]},{"text":"Familiar words have their own training history to partly support them, so they lose less when phonology is weak.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p029":[{"text":"Like the **triangle model** of reading, this account has **no special rules** for the past tense, just **mappings between phonology and semantics**.","children":[]},{"text":"There is **no separate lexicon** that stores the exceptions, so the ideas of **rule** and **exception** only approximately describe the system.","children":[]},{"text":"The account rests on an **association between irregular forms and semantic processing** and an **association between wug test performance and phonological processing**.","children":["Each association links a type of past tense to the system the model says it depends on."]}],"m3-morphology-p030":[],"m3-thought-p001":[],"m3-thought-p002":[{"text":"**Linguistic relativity**, also called the **Sapir-Whorf hypothesis**, is the idea that differences between languages influence how their speakers organize and think about the world.","children":[]},{"text":"**Sapir** argued that speakers of different languages live in **distinct worlds**, not the same world with different labels attached. **Whorf** argued that we **dissect nature along lines laid down by our native language**, because the flux of impressions from the world has to be organized largely by the linguistic systems of our minds.","children":["The **strong** reading says language **determines** what a person can think, while a **weaker** reading says habitual language use only **influences** attention, memory or preferred ways of representing a situation."]}],"m3-thought-p003":[{"text":"Even **Sapir and Whorf** did not necessarily hold the **strong relativist position** that their famous quotations suggest.","children":[]},{"text":"In the first half of the twentieth century, linguistics was really part of **anthropology**, so treating language as part of **culture** seemed more natural than it does to linguists today.","children":["Because language and culture travel together, a difference between two language communities may come from culture or experience rather than from the language itself, so a good study has to separate them."]},{"text":"The studies that follow test relativity mainly in **space** and in the relationship between **space and time**, because these domains allow narrow claims that can be tested.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p004":[{"text":"Saying **where the ball is** requires a **frame of reference**, which is the system that decides what counts as front, left or a fixed direction.","children":[]},{"text":"The same ball can be located relative to the viewer, relative to the chair's own front, or relative to a direction that depends on neither, so the answer depends on which frame the speaker picks.","children":["The ball does not move at all, yet its description changes, because changing the frame changes the relation being described."]}],"m3-thought-p005":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues** distinguish three frames of reference for the same scene of a ball beside a chair.","children":[]},{"text":"The **intrinsic frame** uses the object's own orientation, as in “The ball is at the chair's front.” The **absolute frame** uses a fixed direction, as in “The ball is north of the chair.”","children":["The **relative frame** uses the observer's viewpoint, as in “The ball is to the left of the chair (from my viewpoint).”"]},{"text":"If the observer turns around, relative left and right swap sides, but **north stays north**, so the absolute description does not change.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p006":[{"text":"Most languages describe nearby things **egocentrically**, meaning relative to the speaker's own body, such as to your left or right, in front of you or behind you.","children":[]},{"text":"**Tzeltal**, a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas, does not, because it uses **absolute directions** even for nearby things.","children":["A small number of other languages, mostly unrelated to one another and spread widely, in places such as Bali and Queensland, also use absolute directions."]},{"text":"For most English speakers, a request to raise your east hand does not feel intuitive, because their language names body sides as left and right rather than by compass direction.","children":["This contrast raises the question of whether the way a language habitually describes space relates to how its speakers remember space without words."]}],"m3-thought-p007":[{"text":"Tzeltal has a historically **landmark-based** system in which things are **uphill** or **downhill** from one another, not to the left or right of the speaker or listener.","children":[]},{"text":"In fact, uphill and downhill are used as **cardinal directions**, the fixed directions such as north and south, so Tzeltal is an **absolute system**.","children":["The terms come historically from the slope of the land, but speakers use them as fixed directions rather than pointing to the nearest hill each time."]},{"text":"Because the direction stays fixed when the speaker turns, a position is described the same way from any orientation.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p008":[{"text":"New Yorkers use **uptown** and **downtown** as if they were cardinal directions, but these terms do not work outside Manhattan, so they form a truly **landmark-based system**.","children":[]},{"text":"Manhattan also sits at an angle to **magnetic north**, so the subway map leaves New Yorkers confused about real north and south.","children":["A local direction system is useful only where its landmark exists, so it is not an absolute frame that works everywhere."]},{"text":"The Manhattan case is an aside about reference systems, not an experiment about language and thought.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p009":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues** describe the **chips task**, a **nonlinguistic test** of whether people remember space in **relative** or **absolute** terms, in which the response itself uses no words.","children":[]},{"text":"A participant sees a card with a large dot and a small dot on one table, waits, is turned around to face the opposite way, and then picks the matching card out of four cards on a second table.","children":["Because the person has turned around, the card that keeps the dots the same relative to the body differs from the card that keeps them the same relative to north."]},{"text":"**Dutch speakers** gave **overwhelmingly relative** responses and **Tzeltal speakers** gave **mostly absolute** responses, matching each language's preferred frame.","children":["Language matches the pattern here, but the comparison does not separate language from all other experience."]}],"m3-thought-p010":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues** also used a harder **transitive inference** task, in which a person combines two learned relations to work out a third one.","children":[]},{"text":"At the first table a person sees a cube and a cone, then turns around and sees the cube and a cylinder at a second table. After turning back to the first table, where only the cone remains, the person must place the cylinder.","children":["A relative coder and an absolute coder put the cylinder on opposite sides of the cone, because the person has turned between the two displays."]},{"text":"**Dutch speakers** again gave **mostly relative** answers, and **Tzeltal speakers** gave **mostly absolute** answers.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p011":[{"text":"**Li and Gleitman** pointed out that the Tzeltal speakers were tested **outside**, with **visible, familiar landmarks**, while typical psychology studies are run in **windowless rooms**.","children":[]},{"text":"A setting full of landmarks makes landmark-based coding easy, and a windowless room makes viewer-based coding more convenient.","children":["So the language difference could be **confounded** with the testing setting, meaning the two change together and either one could explain the result."]},{"text":"They tried to **induce landmark-based reasoning in English speakers**, because changing the setting while keeping the language the same tests whether the setting alone can shift the response.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p012":[{"text":"**Li and Gleitman** tested English speakers in three settings, a room with the **blinds down**, a room with the **blinds up** and a view of the street, and **outdoors**.","children":[]},{"text":"With the blinds down, English speakers **overwhelmingly used relative position**, like the Dutch speakers in earlier studies. With the blinds up or outdoors, their relative preference was **less pronounced**.","children":["In those landmark-rich settings, participants tended to split into people giving all absolute or all relative answers, and they asked far more often what the task wanted."]},{"text":"Because only the setting changed, the **environment can shift** the frame people use, although the change is a weaker preference rather than a full switch to absolute coding.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p013":[],"m3-thought-p014":[{"text":"If the language groups were tested in different conditions, the difference between them may be **confounded** with those conditions, meaning the setting changed along with the language and could explain the result.","children":[]},{"text":"The bias toward **relative spatial reasoning** might then be an **artifact**, a result produced by the testing method, of the way psychology tests are usually run in universities.","children":["So an uncontrolled comparison between groups cannot tell a language effect apart from an effect of the testing conditions."]}],"m3-thought-p015":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues** were not impressed by the result from Li and Gleitman.","children":[]},{"text":"They argued that Li and Gleitman made it **too transparent** that rotation was being tested, because they **reduced memory load** and shortened the **time and distance between stimulus and test**.","children":["When a task is easy and obvious, people can choose a response on purpose instead of using their habitual spatial coding."]},{"text":"They also argued that the participants were **Ivy League students**, who excel at working out what the experimenter wants and then making it happen.","children":["This is a **demand characteristics** concern, meaning that participants' guesses about the purpose of a study can change their answers."]}],"m3-thought-p016":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues** re-ran the **animals in a row** experiment with **Dutch college students**, but they changed three things.","children":[]},{"text":"They added a **fourth animal** and used a **longer delay**, both to **increase memory load**, and they put the stimulus and response tables **farther apart** to make the rotation less noticeable.","children":[]},{"text":"Under these harder and less transparent conditions, a **bias toward relative framing remained**.","children":["So the landmark effect depends on how the task is set up, and when memory demands are real the relative preference holds."]}],"m3-thought-p017":[{"text":"For speakers of languages that use **relative frames**, the bias toward relative over absolute frames is **robust to the environment** under two conditions.","children":[]},{"text":"The task must have a **significant memory component**, and the experimenter's interest in rotation and frames of reference must **not be obvious**.","children":[]},{"text":"The importance of memory muddies the waters, because a task that looks spatial may be solved partly with **verbal strategies** while the person holds the information in mind.","children":["So the same nonverbal response could come from a verbal memory as well as a spatial one."]}],"m3-thought-p018":[{"text":"Memory muddies the waters because participants may **recode the animals** in **working memory**, the short-term store that holds information while it is used. They may store the animals as a spoken list such as “lion, tiger, bear,” and then naturally lay the list out **from left to right**.","children":[]},{"text":"A list keeps the order of the animals but not their compass direction, so rebuilding it left to right produces a **relative-looking** answer without any relative spatial memory.","children":[]},{"text":"Left to right seems a natural way to lay out a sequence in time because English is **read and written from left to right**, a possibility that later studies of reading direction test.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p019":[{"text":"The lyric by Brown and Byrd, “You're like a dull knife, just ain't cuttin',” uses **metaphorical language**, which describes one kind of experience with words from another.","children":[]},{"text":"Failing to make a compelling argument is “not cutting” because a dull knife fails at its job, and “talking loud” but “saying nothing” needs no literally loud voice, since it means talking a lot without making a point.","children":["Metaphors like these let physical words express **abstract concepts**, which raises the question of how much of ordinary language is truly non-metaphorical."]},{"text":"Arguments about language and thought can generate more heat than light, so the studies that follow ask a narrow question, whether language affects how people think about **time in terms of space**.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p020":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues** point out that both English and Mandarin use **horizontal front and back terms** for time, as when English speakers look forward to good times ahead or think back to troubles behind them.","children":[]},{"text":"Mandarin speakers also **systematically and frequently use vertical terms**, so earlier events are **shàng** (“up”) and later events are **xià** (“down”).","children":["The phrase “shàng ge yuè” means last month and “xià ge yuè” means next month, while the same words also mean climbing and descending in space.","In one corpus of written Mandarin, **more than a third** of the spatial metaphors for time were vertical."]},{"text":"This difference in talk motivates testing whether Mandarin speakers also **think about time vertically**.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p021":[{"text":"**Boroditsky** ran a **priming** experiment, in which a first task makes a related later task faster or slower.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants first read a **horizontal** spatial prime such as “The black worm is ahead of the white worm” or a **vertical** one such as “The black ball is above the white ball,” and then answered **purely temporal earlier or later questions**.","children":["**English speakers** answered **faster after horizontal primes**, while **Mandarin speakers** answered **faster after vertical primes**."]},{"text":"English speakers given **short training on vertical metaphors** for time, such as “Cars were invented above fax machines,” then answered **faster after vertical primes**, so the effect can shift with brief experience.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p022":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini** offered an alternative explanation for the vertical time effect in Mandarin, the **direction of text**.","children":[]},{"text":"English is written in left-to-right lines, Chinese is **mostly left-first** but is sometimes written in vertical columns, and Taiwanese text is **mostly right-first** in vertical columns.","children":["So a vertical time effect could come from **reading habits** rather than from vertical metaphors in the language."]},{"text":"When people arranged items in order, every English speaker arranged them **left to right**, most Chinese speakers did too, and Taiwanese speakers were split across **left to right, right to left and top to bottom**.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p023":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues** controlled for reading direction by **excluding Chinese readers who reported reading top to bottom**.","children":[]},{"text":"**English speakers** were faster with the **left-is-earlier** key mapping than with the right-is-earlier mapping, but they showed **no clear advantage** for either vertical mapping.","children":["**Mandarin speakers** were faster with the **left-is-earlier** mapping and also with the **top-is-earlier** mapping."]},{"text":"Because the vertical effect remained without the top-to-bottom readers, that reading habit **cannot fully explain** it.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p024":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini** asked people to push a **blue button for past events** and a **red button for future events**, or the reverse in different blocks.","children":[]},{"text":"Because the colors switched sides, the **past** button was **on the left half the time** and **on the right** the other half, so color cannot explain a difference between mappings.","children":["With normal text, people were **faster when past was on the left and future was on the right**, in both blocks."]},{"text":"This advantage fits an implicit **mental timeline** that runs from earlier on the left to later on the right, and it appears even though the judgment is about time rather than space.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p025":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini** then had people do a few minutes of **mirror reading**, which means reading text printed backward, before doing the same past and future button task.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **first block** the usual advantage for the left-earlier mapping stayed about the same.","children":["By the **second block** the effect **reversed**, so people were **faster when earlier was on the right and later was on the left**."]},{"text":"Because brief reading experience flipped the mapping, **reading direction contributes** to how people lay out time in space, and the mapping is **flexible** rather than fixed by native language alone.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p026":[{"text":"The passage about snow words comes from a classic rant by **Pullum** called “The Great Eskimo Vocabulary Hoax,” and he comes down **against** the exaggerated relativist story.","children":["The word Eskimo is not an acceptable term for the Inuktitut people, whose language is the most likely source of the examples, and it appears only because it is the title."]},{"text":"**Boas** originally noted that English uses separate roots for forms of water, such as lake, river, rain and foam, just as Eskimo uses separate roots such as aput ‘snow on the ground’ and qana ‘falling snow.’","children":["Boas's point was simply that English expresses these ideas with phrases built on the root snow, but things could have been otherwise."]},{"text":"**Whorf** later picked up the example of Boas and used it vaguely in an amateur article, and it grew into an **exaggerated story** about vocabulary and thought.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p027":[],"m3-modalities-p001":[],"m3-modalities-p002":[{"text":"**Signed languages** and **Braille** are really different cases, so they should not be treated as the same kind of thing.","children":[]},{"text":"A signed language is a **natural language** with its own **linguistic structure**, while Braille is a **system for reading by touch** that lets blind people read a spoken language they share with sighted people.","children":["So changing the modality of a whole language is not the same as changing how a written language is read."]},{"text":"They are grouped together because both reveal the **plasticity of sensory systems**, which is how the brain gets organized differently when language input comes from a different modality.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p003":[{"text":"**Emmorey and colleagues** list the obvious ways **sign languages** differ from spoken languages, which are **modality**, **articulators**, **use of space** and **iconicity**.","children":["The modality is visual, the articulators are the hands, face and upper body working together, and positions in space can carry relations between words."]},{"text":"**Iconicity** means that the form of a sign resembles its meaning, but it is **easy to exaggerate** when individual words are viewed with their **glosses**.","children":["A gloss is the written translation given for a sign, so it makes the resemblance seem more obvious than it is, and a learner still has to learn the forms and structure of the language."]}],"m3-modalities-p004":[{"text":"Fluent signing **coordinates the hands, face and upper body**, and signers keep **eye contact** with each other while they sign.","children":[]},{"text":"Signing is **fluid** and **fast**, so it is hard for a learner to **segment words**, meaning to find where one sign ends and the next begins.","children":["The same is true of fluent speech, where word boundaries are also not obvious to a learner."]},{"text":"So the thing to study is coordinated language production across all of these articulators, not one isolated hand shape.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p005":[{"text":"As with speech, sign has **featural descriptions**, so the **phonology of sign language** can be described with features, as **Emmorey and colleagues** did.","children":[]},{"text":"Signs can **differ contrastively** in **hand configuration**, **location** of the gesture and **movement**, which means that changing just one of these features changes which sign it is.","children":["“please” and “sorry” form one such pair of signs, and “onion” and “apple” form another pair."]},{"text":"So phonology is about the organization of contrastive form, and it is not limited to sound.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p006":[{"text":"The research question is whether there is **categorical perception** for these sign dimensions, meaning whether people sort a smooth physical change into sharp categories and tell apart differences best at the category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"To test it, hand configuration was changed in small steps along a **continuum** of images.","children":["The **phonemic** continuum ran from the sign PLEASE to the sign SORRY, so it crossed a change in which sign it is.","The **allophonic** continuum ran from “open-N” to “closed N” in the sign SAY-NO-TO, so the hand changed but the sign stayed the same."]},{"text":"Signers identified each image and discriminated pairs of images, and the answer was **yes for the phonemic continuum only**.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p007":[{"text":"For **deaf signers**, the **phonemic** continuum produced a **fairly sharp categorical boundary** between the fourth and sixth stimuli, and this boundary lines up with the **peak in the discrimination function**.","children":["So hand shapes that crossed the line between PLEASE and SORRY were the easiest to tell apart, which is the familiar pattern from speech sounds."]},{"text":"In the **allophonic** condition, the **identification function** was **much shallower**, so many more stimuli were ambiguous, and there was **no peak in discrimination**.","children":[]},{"text":"Since only the contrast that changes sign identity is perceived this way, signers show **categorical perception** for some contrastive features of sign.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p008":[{"text":"Sign language uses **space** to show the **relationships between words** in a sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"In a very transparent case, the **relative position of two objects** is given by **where the classifier sign is made**.","children":["A **classifier sign** is a sign that represents an object, so making it at a certain spot in signing space places that object at that spot in the scene."]},{"text":"So understanding sentences of any complexity requires **keeping track of where signs were made**, and spatial processing becomes part of understanding the language.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p009":[{"text":"**Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters** found that brain activity during **sign language comprehension** has important similarities with spoken language and important differences from it.","children":[]},{"text":"Like speech, sign shows **left lateralization** and activates the **classical language areas**, which are **Broca's area** in the inferior frontal region and **Wernicke's area** in the posterior superior temporal region.","children":[]},{"text":"Unlike speech, sign also activates **visual areas** and **superior parietal** areas, which are classic **spatial areas**.","children":["They compared deaf signers, hearing signers and hearing people perceiving audio-visual speech, so the extra visual and spatial activity reflects the demands of a seen, spatial signal."]}],"m3-modalities-p010":[{"text":"**Bavelier and colleagues** asked whether **deafness**, **experience with sign**, or both change **spatial attention**.","children":[]},{"text":"They compared **hearing controls**, **deaf signers** and **hearing signers**, who watched dots and responded to brief changes in **luminance** (brightness) or **velocity** (speed of motion).","children":["Attention was directed either to the **center** of the screen or to the **periphery**, which is the outer edge of the visual field."]},{"text":"**Deaf participants** had an **advantage** in this task only when attention was directed to the **periphery**.","children":["Including hearing signers separates signing experience from the lack of hearing, because both groups know sign but only one group is deaf."]}],"m3-modalities-p011":[{"text":"**Learning sign language** changes the **laterality** of the motion perception areas, labeled **MT/MST**, from the right side of the brain to the left side.","children":["This happens in both deaf and hearing signers, perhaps because motion perception needs to “talk to” the **left-lateralized language networks** in people who learn sign."]},{"text":"**Enhanced peripheral visual attention**, however, is found **only in deaf individuals** and not in hearing signers.","children":["Perhaps this is because hearing people depend more on **auditory cues** for spatial attention when things are at the far periphery."]},{"text":"So signing experience and deafness have **two different effects** on visual processing.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p012":[{"text":"To separate **language processing** from the visual, spatial and motor processing of **hand movements**, sign comprehension was compared directly with **tic-tac**.","children":[]},{"text":"Tic-tac is a sophisticated and outmoded **gestural system** that was used to communicate about odds at horse races in the UK.","children":["Like sign, it uses organized, sequenced hand movements that carry meaning, but it is not a natural language."]},{"text":"So any difference in brain activity between the two reflects **language** rather than simply watching hands move.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p013":[{"text":"**Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters** found **stronger activity around Wernicke's area**, in the **posterior superior temporal** region, for sign language than for tic-tac.","children":["Since both involve organized, sequenced hand movements, the extra activity reflects processing a **natural language** rather than processing moving hands."]},{"text":"The frontal activation was **superior to Broca's area**, meaning it lies above that area, so it should not be described as a Broca's area effect.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p014":[{"text":"**Sign language has phonological structure**, and native signers show **categorical perception** for some contrastive features.","children":[]},{"text":"**Learning a signed language** leads to some **brain reorganization**, particularly in the **lateralization of motion processing**.","children":["Attention to the visual periphery is critical for both hearing and deaf signers, but only **deaf signers** show **enhanced visual attention in the periphery**."]},{"text":"Compared with non-language stimuli that also involve organized, sequenced hand movements, signed sentences activate the **posterior STG** (superior temporal gyrus), which is **Wernicke's area**.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p015":[{"text":"Understanding a **signed spatial description** can require a **perspective transformation**, which is mentally turning the scene around.","children":[]},{"text":"The location of a table within a room can be described in sign from the **speaker's perspective** or from the **addressee's perspective**.","children":["The speaker's version uses the signs I-ENTER, TABLE and THERE, and the addressee's version uses YOU-ENTER, TABLE and THERE, while the table stays in the same spot by the entrance."]},{"text":"Signers show **better comprehension** for room descriptions from the **speaker's perspective**, even though that description requires the mental transformation.","children":["So the best understood description is not the one that avoids every spatial transformation."]}],"m3-modalities-p016":[{"text":"**Braille reading** is in many ways **equivalent to reading print**, because the reader is still reading a written form of a spoken language.","children":[]},{"text":"One difference is that Braille symbols are **optimized for tactile discrimination**, meaning telling shapes apart by touch, so reading Braille **by sight is really hard**.","children":[]},{"text":"Another difference is that the **fingers take in letters one at a time**, so the letters arrive in a sequence, which is a different demand from viewing printed text.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p017":[{"text":"**Reading Braille activates visual cortex** in blind individuals, even though the input comes through touch.","children":[]},{"text":"When **early blind** participants are compared with **late blind** participants, the **earlier sight is lost**, the **more extensive** the visual activity to tactile stimuli.","children":[]},{"text":"The **visual word form area**, a region tied to processing written words, is **strongly activated in early blind** participants, more so than in late blind participants.","children":["So the usual sense of a brain region does not fully decide what it processes, and the timing of sight loss shapes how the brain is organized."]}],"m3-modalities-p018":[{"text":"Earlier, the question came up whether **competition** from hearing could be preventing hearing individuals from developing enhanced **peripheral visual attention**, and this study tests competition directly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Merabet and colleagues** had **sighted** people wear **blindfolds for five days** and trained them to **read Braille**, while a control group was trained without a blindfold.","children":[]},{"text":"Relative to the control group, the **blindfolded group learned more quickly**.","children":["So taking away competing visual input sped up tactile learning, even over a short time in sighted adults."]}],"m3-modalities-p019":[{"text":"Over the course of the experiment, **Merabet and colleagues** found that sighted, blindfolded participants began to show **some activity in primary visual cortex** when **reading Braille**.","children":[]},{"text":"When the **blindfold was removed** on the sixth day, this effect was **completely reversed**.","children":["So the change in visual cortex was **rapid and reversible**, which fits uncovering inputs that already existed rather than building a new pathway in a few days."]}],"m3-modalities-p020":[{"text":"**Transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS)**, which disrupts activity in a targeted brain area, was applied to **visual cortex**, and it **disrupted Braille reading in the blindfolded group only**.","children":["On the fifth day, the blindfolded group's **error rate rose** under TMS compared with baseline and sham, but after the blindfold came off this effect was gone."]},{"text":"This is tentative evidence that blindfolded participants were somehow **using visual cortex for tactile processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"One explanation is that visual cortex **gets tactile input all the time**, even in sighted people, but this input is **too weak to compete** with the stronger, more consistent visual input.","children":["So even **short-term removal of visual input** can **unmask** tactile responses in visual cortex."]}],"m3-modalities-p021":[{"text":"**Language circuitry is resilient** across modalities, because **classical language areas** respond to **sign language** despite its very different surface form, and **Braille reading** engages networks important for **processing text**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Cross-modal plasticity**, in which brain areas for one sense take on input from another, suggests that brain organization is shaped over development by **competition**.","children":["When auditory input is unavailable, areas thought to be auditory are used to process sign, and visual attention is enhanced at the periphery."]},{"text":"The same idea of competing cognitive and perceptual processes appeared in the **Competition Model** of second language learning.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p001":[],"m3-review-p002":[{"text":"**Phonemes** are not the same thing as letters, and letters are not the same thing as phonemes. A **mispronouncing contest**, in which people willfully mispronounced words such as COLONEL and PARADIGM, helped make this point.","children":["These words are hard because their spelling does not reliably tell a reader how they sound."]},{"text":"Scripts differ in how regular their spelling is, and this difference is called **orthographic depth**. Pinyin for Mandarin and French are relatively regular, but English is a relatively **deep** script because of its many irregularities.","children":["Finnish and Korean are very **shallow** scripts, so their spelling predicts sound closely."]}],"m3-review-p003":[{"text":"Finnish is a very **transparent** writing system, which is also called **shallow** under the **orthographic depth hypothesis**. Each letter corresponds more or less unambiguously to one **phoneme**, so a reader can work out the pronunciation from the spelling.","children":["Doubled letters mark **geminated** consonants, which are held for longer, or **long vowels**."]},{"text":"Because the spelling is so regular, **Finnish children** learn to decode pronunciations for written forms very quickly.","children":["A short Finnish lesson was played as an example, and the speaker suggests that listeners pick up the idea within about a minute. The lesson is a demonstration, so it supports the spelling claims rather than any recovered recording."]}],"m3-review-p004":[{"text":"English is a bit of an **outlier** among languages that use the Roman alphabet. As a result, kids learn to **decode** more slowly in English than they do in most other languages.","children":[]},{"text":"In first grade, children learning a wide variety of other languages generate plausible pronunciations for **pseudowords** at a much higher rate than English learners. A pseudoword is a made-up word that follows the spelling and sound patterns of its language.","children":["Because a pseudoword is not a familiar stored word, the child has to generalize from learned spelling and sound patterns. The lower English performance fits with English being a deep script, and the test is about decoding skill, not about the child's whole language ability."]}],"m3-review-p005":[{"text":"Korean is even more regular than Finnish, and similar **phonemes** are written with similar symbols. The letter shapes therefore carry information about sound.","children":["The basic velar consonant letters share an angular shape, and extra strokes or doubling mark related sounds."]},{"text":"The question asks which orthographic feature matches **velar place of articulation**, which means the place where the back of the tongue touches the soft palate. The answer is the angular shape that the velar consonants share.","children":["Korean letters are grouped into syllable blocks with **initial**, **medial** and sometimes **final** positions, so the blocks are not unrelated pictures of whole words."]}],"m3-review-p006":[{"text":"This is a **landmark finding** in psycholinguistics, and it has shaped later work on areas such as inflectional morphology. The **frequency effect** is much larger for **exception words** than for regular words.","children":["An exception word has a pronunciation that breaks the usual spelling pattern."]},{"text":"**Naming latency**, the time needed to begin saying a word, is much longer for low-frequency exception words than for low-frequency regular words. High-frequency words show a much smaller regularity difference, so the two kinds of word come close together.","children":["An **interaction** means the effect of one factor depends on the other, so the regularity cost is not added equally to high-frequency and low-frequency words."]}],"m3-review-p007":[{"text":"One interpretation says there are **two separate processes** in word recognition. The first is a **sublexical process**, which is decoding, and the second is a **lexical process**, which is knowledge of a particular word.","children":[]},{"text":"For **high-frequency words**, the lexical process is so fast and efficient that the sublexical process has little influence. The sublexical process appears mainly when a reader sees **nonwords** or **low-frequency words**.","children":["A low-frequency exception is slower to retrieve, so the regularized output from decoding can interfere with it. This is an interpretation of the interaction, not the interaction itself, so other architectures must be tested against the same pattern."]}],"m3-review-p008":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland** built **PDP models** of visual word recognition, and these models make different assumptions from dual-route models. In these models, **rules** are generalizations learned from many words, not a separate process.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexicon** is also **distributed**, so some whole-word information is stored in the spelling to sound mappings and some is stored in other parts of the system. Their research strategy differs as well. They explain reading with **domain-general learning mechanisms** instead of reverse-engineering the system from adult and neuropsychological data.","children":["Distributed knowledge does not mean the model has no memory for individual words, because distributed refers to how knowledge is spread across the network."]}],"m3-review-p009":[{"text":"The model captured the **frequency by regularity interaction** without assuming that frequency and regularity result from different processes.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regular** words are consistent with statistical patterns that many other words share, while **exception** words are inconsistent with those patterns. Frequency also affects how well words are learned, so highly frequent words are less affected by inconsistency with other words.","children":["A low-frequency exception has both weak item-specific learning and poor support from the dominant pattern. That combination creates a larger disadvantage than either factor alone."]}],"m3-review-p010":[{"text":"In principle, a PDP model can read **nonwords** with the same set of weights it uses for words. Generalization takes advantage of the same statistical patterns that give rise to regularity effects.","children":[]},{"text":"A new spelling activates patterns that overlap with familiar spellings, so the network can generalize to it. No stored entry for that nonword is required, so this is proposed as a way to decode without a separate **grapheme-to-phoneme rule** module.","children":["Novel pronunciation comes from applying learned statistical structure, so nonword reading does not automatically require a separate symbolic route."]}],"m3-review-p011":[{"text":"In practice, though, the model was not very good at **nonwords**, so its nonword accuracy was weak. This leaves an **ambiguity in model evaluation**. The failure could mean the theoretical claim was wrong, or it could mean that something specific to the way the model was built was wrong.","children":[]},{"text":"The theoretical claim is that nonwords and exceptions can be read by the same associative mechanism. **Poor nonword performance** can challenge either that claim or a particular implementation. One possibility is that a single associative mechanism cannot explain the task. Another possibility is that the chosen representations fail to capture the similarities needed for generalization.","children":["Improving the implementation while keeping the mechanism helps separate these two possibilities. A better representation is a different move from adding a separate nonword route."]}],"m3-review-p012":[{"text":"Many studies find a relationship between **decoding**, which is nonword reading, and **phonological processing**. The phonological representations used by **Seidenberg and McClelland** were unusual, and they were called **Wickelfeatures**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues** improved the phonological representations, and their model produced better performance on nonwords. This supports a representational explanation for at least part of the earlier failure.","children":["Changing the representation can improve decoding without adding a symbolic rule system, which matters when judging the original theoretical claim."]}],"m3-review-p013":[{"text":"**Generalization depends on similarity**, and this principle was inherited from behaviorism. Poor phonological representations, such as Wickelfeatures, fail to capture the similarity space of words in the language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Word-specific information** holds up better under poor representations, because it does not depend entirely on generalization. A familiar word keeps support from its own training history, while a nonword lacks that item-specific experience and depends more heavily on generalization.","children":["Unfamiliar forms are therefore especially vulnerable to poor representations, and no dedicated nonword mechanism is needed to explain this. A weaker shared representation and damage to a separate route both create a task difference, but they point to different explanations."]}],"m3-review-p014":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg** studied this more systematically by manipulating the amount of **noise** in the phonological representations. Increasing noise makes the sound representations less reliable, while the general learning framework stays the same.","children":[]},{"text":"The manipulation reproduced several findings from research on developmental dyslexia. It produced poorer nonword decoding, a relative sparing of word recognition, and a link between the severity of the phonological deficit and the degree of decoding difficulty.","children":["Nonwords are especially dependent on reliable generalization, so degraded shared representations can create a reading pattern that looks selective. Noise is the manipulated property, and word and nonword performance are the outcomes, so the simulation tests a directional mechanism rather than only observing a correlation."]}],"m3-review-p015":[{"text":"The **Pinker and Ullman** account is a **dual route** model for the past tense, and it raises the question of how it is similar to and different from the dual-route model for reading. The lexicon supplies irregular forms such as HELD, while **grammatical combination** can produce WALK plus -ed.","children":[]},{"text":"Like dual-route reading, this account separates word-specific knowledge from a productive operation. Unlike reading aloud, its task is to transform a stem according to a grammatical feature, not to convert spelling into sound.","children":["The input includes a stem and a past-tense feature, and a **blocking** connection keeps a retrieved irregular from competing unchecked with a regular form."]}],"m3-review-p016":[{"text":"The **Words and Rules** proposal was motivated partly by a theory of grammar. Dual-route theories of reading were strongly motivated by **neuropsychological double dissociations**, while **Pinker and colleagues** were concerned with categorical, **symbolic** grammars.","children":[]},{"text":"Those grammars describe productive patterns, but these rules often have exceptions. The morphology proposal keeps the grammatical rule system distinct from exceptional lexical information.","children":["So the two architectures look similar, but the reading model came from patient dissociations and the past-tense model came from an argument about grammar."]}],"m3-review-p017":[{"text":"Rather than make the grammar inelegant by adding machinery for exceptions, linguists often left the exceptions out of the grammar. The **dual-process** model instead handles exceptions with a second process, which keeps the rule grammar elegant, symbolic and categorical.","children":[]},{"text":"This second process had all the properties that the rule route did not have. It was **word-specific**, so it is lexical, and it was **sensitive to frequency** of occurrence. It was also **sensitive to phonological similarity**, as in KEEP and KEPT, WEEP and WEPT, and LEAP and LEAPT.","children":["An exception can belong to a small family of similar words rather than being unrelated to other words. Assigning these properties to lexical memory lets the rule system stay categorical and general."]}],"m3-review-p018":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland** offered an alternative in which all of the learning is associative. They modeled the past tense as a learned association between present and past sound patterns, so a single trainable network learns across verbs instead of applying a separately specified -ed rule.","children":[]},{"text":"One learning mechanism is proposed for both regularities and exceptions. Regularity is a pattern learned from many examples, and irregular forms are learned within the same system.","children":["The model encodes the phonology of a root, transforms that representation through modifiable connections, and decodes an output pattern, so the fixed encoding and decoding stages surround a modifiable pattern associator."]}],"m3-review-p019":[{"text":"**U-shaped learning** describes an early success, then a decline, and then a later recovery. Children often get worse before they get better, so their performance goes down before it goes back up.","children":["A child may correctly say RAN, later produce RUNNED, and eventually return to RAN."]},{"text":"The middle error is a **regularization**, because the child applies the common past-tense pattern to an irregular verb.","children":["The U shape describes accuracy over the course of development, not the shape of a word, and development is what the model was built to capture."]}],"m3-review-p020":[{"text":"The **Words and Rules** account explains U-shaped learning through changes in the use of rules and lexical exceptions. Early correct forms are imitations that do not depend on the same mechanisms as adult processing.","children":[]},{"text":"When a rule is first learned, it is applied to all cases, so it works as the best hypothesis for items in general. Over time, exceptions are learned one by one and become part of lexical knowledge, and then a **blocking process** develops.","children":["Blocking means that retrieving the right irregular prevents the otherwise available regular form, so overregularization is productive rule use that exceptions have not yet constrained."]}],"m3-review-p021":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland's** model can also produce **U-shaped learning**, because its training set is based on data about the input children receive, and that input changes over time. Early on, irregular verbs predominate, since the highest-frequency verbs tend to have irregular past-tense forms such as GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO.","children":[]},{"text":"As the vocabulary grows to include more regular forms, the model reorganizes to capture those regularities, and this interferes with some of the early-learned forms. Later, the network recovers the irregular responses while keeping broad regularity.","children":["No new symbolic rule component is inserted at the point of decline, so the drop comes from changing weights and changing input."]}],"m3-review-p022":[{"text":"The model was trained on a small number of verbs, so the researchers could run a **wug test** with real words. A real English verb can be novel to the model if it was left out of training, so the test uses the logic of a wug task even when the items are real words to people.","children":[]},{"text":"Testing untrained verbs asks whether the network learned **transferable structure**. Generalization is defined relative to the learner's experience, so a familiar human word still counts as a novel test for the model when it was withheld from training.","children":["The model generates many appropriate past-tense features, but getting features right is different from producing whole words perfectly. A high proportion of correct **Wickelfeatures** does not mean the same proportion of whole words was pronounced perfectly."]}],"m3-review-p023":[{"text":"Some model outputs contain changes that are unlike ordinary human **regularizations**. Examples include a MADE(D)-like change to MATE, and the unusual outputs MEMBLED for MAIL and TOUREDER for TOUR.","children":[]},{"text":"The model authors acknowledge that people are unlikely to make some of these errors. They suggest that additional output constraints could reduce these bizarre responses.","children":["Capturing an overall learning pattern is different from matching the detailed form of human errors, so a plausible learning curve is not enough if the model produces implausible words."]}],"m3-review-p024":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg** proposed that phonology supports **rule-like generalization**, while irregular forms require greater support from **semantics**. In this proposal, the same system links speech input, meaning and speech output.","children":[]},{"text":"Reliable sound representations support extension from familiar present and past pairs to new forms. Irregular mappings receive additional support from the particular word's semantic representation.","children":["The proposal concerns different degrees of dependence on shared systems, not a separate symbolic rule route for every regular response."]}],"m3-review-p025":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg** aimed to explain a **dissociation** in neuropsychological data, in which irregular and nonword past-tense performance come apart. These tasks differ in how much they depend on learned word-specific information and on phonological generalization.","children":[]},{"text":"A nonword has no familiar item-specific semantic history to support its inflection, so it depends on generalization. A known irregular can need semantic support because its output is not well predicted by the dominant pattern.","children":["Damaging phonological or semantic integrity tests whether these different dependencies can produce opposite impairment profiles, and the same architecture has to explain both sides of the pattern."]}],"m3-review-p026":[{"text":"Patients with **Broca's aphasia** have injury to left anterior brain regions and poor speech production. Their **regular morphology** is especially poor for nonwords, so they struggle to say that wug becomes wugged and blick becomes blicked.","children":[]},{"text":"A novel BLICK must be transformed without relying on a familiar stored past tense, which makes nonword inflection especially demanding. Regular forms are also vulnerable, but irregular forms are relatively better in this comparison.","children":["This difficulty is connected to sound processing, so it motivates looking at phonological contributions to the apparent morphological deficit. Nonword accuracy falls below both regular and irregular accuracy."]}],"m3-review-p027":[{"text":"**Wernicke's** and **Alzheimer's** patients, and **anomic** patients with injury to inferior or middle temporal regions, have a larger problem with **irregular** past-tense forms. Their irregular performance is lower than their regular and nonword performance.","children":[]},{"text":"This creates the other side of the dissociation, and it motivates examining the information that especially supports known irregular forms.","children":["The irregular condition is the lowest of the three, so relatively preserved nonword inflection can exist alongside poor irregular inflection. The diagnostic labels do not make every individual's performance identical."]}],"m3-review-p028":[{"text":"The classical contrast says that **Wernicke's aphasia** involves fluent but often poorly interpretable speech, sometimes described as **word salad**, with neologisms, while syntax stays roughly intact. **Broca's aphasia** involves **telegraphic speech**, with difficulty with word order and grammatical particles, although content words are relatively spared.","children":[]},{"text":"This contrast is attractive because it seems to separate word problems from grammar problems. Fluency, grammatical organization and meaningful word selection are different dimensions of speech, and each example profile emphasizes a different disruption.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p029":[{"text":"Wernicke's aphasia is described as a problem with activation in the **lexicon**, possibly with inhibition, and Broca's aphasia is described as a problem with **grammar**. This is fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca's actual patients would not necessarily be classified as having Broca's aphasia today. They were arguably unable to produce speech at all, apparently because of motor control problems, so they would be classified as having **apraxia of speech**, which concerns planning or coordinating speech movements.","children":["So the modern label names a grammar problem, while Broca's own patients had a problem with the motor control of speech."]}],"m3-review-p030":[{"text":"The Wernicke and Broca dichotomy lines up with a sharp distinction from late twentieth century formal linguistics between the **grammar** and the **lexicon**. This goes back to Chomsky's formulation of the computational problem of language learning and to demonstrations of the independence of syntax and semantics.","children":[]},{"text":"A grammar combines forms, while lexical knowledge concerns words and their properties. **Colorless green ideas sleep furiously** is grammatical but semantically unusual, so syntactic well-formedness cannot be reduced to whether a sentence describes a sensible event.","children":["That example contrasts with a word order that breaks English syntax, which isolates grammatical organization from familiarity or plausibility."]}],"m3-review-p031":[{"text":"The **functional level** selects word meanings and semantic roles, while the **positional level** organizes word forms into a grammatical sequence. Phonological encoding and articulation come later in producing an utterance.","children":[]},{"text":"A **lemma** is a representation used in lexical selection, which is different from simply executing its sound pattern. Lemmas have an arbitrary relationship to their referents, while the syntactic trees and affixes at the positional level are products of abstract syntactic processes.","children":["Words such as HOUSEBOAT and DOGHOUSE have meanings that are clearly not arbitrary, and speech errors suggest that derivational morphology is done at the positional level."]},{"text":"Word selection, ordering, sound encoding and articulation are different levels of explanation, so an apparent lexical and grammatical contrast should be linked to those operations carefully. The proposal runs from conceptual preparation through the functional and positional levels to phonological encoding and speech gestures.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p032":[{"text":"In most languages, people describe nearby space **egocentrically**, using the speaker's own position and orientation. Left, right, in front of you and behind you are familiar examples.","children":[]},{"text":"**Tzeltal**, a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas, does not do this. Tzeltal and a small number of other languages, which are mostly unrelated to one another and broadly distributed, use **absolute directions** instead.","children":["Tzeltal speakers commonly use absolute directions where English speakers might use these relative terms, so the contrast raises the question of whether habitual language use relates to nonverbal spatial coding."]}],"m3-review-p033":[{"text":"**Tzeltal** has a historically **landmark-based** system, in which things are either uphill or downhill from one another rather than to the left or right of the speaker or listener. The terms **uphill** and **downhill** work as cardinal directions, which makes this an **absolute system**.","children":[]},{"text":"A landmark origin and an absolute use are different properties. A historical connection to a slope does not mean that each use points to the nearest visible hill. A direction is kept as a stable reference rather than recalculated from the speaker's current left and right.","children":["This lets positions be described consistently as the speaker changes orientation, so the contrast is with a description that depends entirely on the viewpoint."]}],"m3-review-p034":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues** used a non-linguistic test to tell **relative** spatial memory from **absolute** spatial memory. A participant sees a configuration, waits, and then chooses its match at a second table after being turned completely around.","children":[]},{"text":"A **relative** response keeps the arrangement in line with the person's viewpoint, while an **absolute** response keeps its orientation in the environment. The two responses diverge after rotation, because the north direction stays fixed while the person turns.","children":["**Dutch speakers** predominantly gave relative responses, while **Tzeltal speakers** gave more absolute responses. The pattern fits their preferred linguistic frames, but it does not isolate language from all other experience."]}],"m3-review-p035":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues** made the task harder by testing **spatial inference across displays**. Participants learn the relation between two objects, rotate to another display, and then learn a second relation that involves a shared object.","children":[]},{"text":"A cube appears in both displays, so it links the cone and cylinder relations. Participants then place one object relative to another using the combined information. Relative and absolute coding lead to different placements because the person's orientation has changed.","children":["**Dutch** and **Tzeltal** response patterns again differ in the direction that matches their preferred spatial frames, and the final placement tells a viewer-relative inference apart from one that keeps environmental direction."]}],"m3-review-p036":[{"text":"**Li and Gleitman** argued that landmark-based similarity can be induced in **English speakers**. The Tzeltal speakers were tested outside, with visible and familiar landmarks, whereas typical psychology lab studies are done in windowless rooms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Testing conditions** could contribute to the apparent language-group difference. An environment with useful landmarks can encourage landmark-based coding, while a windowless laboratory can make viewer-relative descriptions more convenient.","children":["Manipulating landmark access within English speakers tests whether the behavior changes without changing the native language."]},{"text":"A **language comparison** can be confounded with a **setting comparison**, so the environment has to be considered before the whole difference is assigned to linguistic structure. The critical contrast is access to spatial cues, because windows, outdoor views and familiar surroundings change which references are useful.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p037":[{"text":"In the **blinds down** condition, **English speakers** overwhelmingly used relative position in this task, just like the Dutch speakers in earlier studies. With the blinds up and a view of the street, or outdoors, the preference for relative position was less pronounced.","children":[]},{"text":"**Li and Gleitman** found that English speakers' preference for relative coding weakened when external landmarks were available. The manipulation changed the spatial information while keeping the language group the same, so task context can influence the response.","children":["This challenges an explanation in which language alone fixes the frame that people choose."]},{"text":"The relative preference became weaker, which is not a complete switch to absolute coding.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p038":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues** were not impressed. They argued that **Li and Gleitman** made it too transparent that they were testing something about rotation, because the task reduced memory load and the time and distance between stimulus and test.","children":[]},{"text":"Li and Gleitman's participants were Ivy League students, and students like these are very good at working out what the experimenter wants and then giving it.","children":["Participants who infer what is being tested can change their response for reasons other than habitual spatial coding."]},{"text":"This is a **demand characteristic** explanation, in which the participants' reading of the experiment influences the response apart from their normal coding preference. The response focuses on memory load and task transparency, rather than denying that landmarks can ever matter.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p039":[{"text":"The research question is whether there is **categorical perception** for these dimensions in sign language. Categorical perception can be tested by varying a sign feature in small steps, while the observer's identification and discrimination responses are measured.","children":[]},{"text":"The **hand-configuration continuum** moves between forms. One continuum crosses a **phonemic** distinction between the signs PLEASE and SORRY, while the **allophonic** comparison changes hand configuration without the same change in sign identity.","children":["The test separates a physical change from a linguistically meaningful category change, because both continua vary visually but only one crosses the relevant contrast."]},{"text":"A sharp **identification boundary**, with better discrimination near that boundary, would indicate category-sensitive perception. Comparing equal-sized steps within and across a linguistic boundary is more informative than comparing only the two endpoints.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p040":[{"text":"**Deaf signers** show a sharper categorical boundary for phonemic changes than for allophonic changes. In the phonemic condition, identification shifts steeply between the fourth and sixth stimuli, and discrimination is strongest at that same boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"In the allophonic condition, the identification function is much shallower, so many more stimuli are ambiguous, and there is no peak in the discrimination function. Comparable physical steps are easier to tell apart when they cross the category boundary.","children":["The steep identification shift combined with the discrimination peak supports **categorical perception**, because identification alone would give a less complete test."]}],"m3-review-p041":[{"text":"**Campbell and colleagues** compared brain activity during sign language comprehension with activity during spoken language comprehension. Deaf signers, hearing signers and hearing participants who watched audiovisual speech were included, and the comparison found both shared features and differences.","children":[]},{"text":"Sign comprehension and speech comprehension both show **left lateralization** and recruit the classical language areas, namely the inferior frontal region known as Broca's area and the posterior superior temporal region known as Wernicke's area.","children":["Similarity does not require identical activation everywhere."]},{"text":"Signing also recruits more **visual areas** and draws less on auditory areas, and it engages **superior parietal** regions that are linked to spatial processing. The extra visual and spatial activity reflects the demands of processing a signed signal, and hearing signers are useful because signing experience is not limited to people who are deaf.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p042":[{"text":"Sign language has **phonological structure**, and native signers show **categorical perception** for some contrastive features. Learning a signed language also causes some **brain reorganization**, particularly in the **lateralization of motion processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"Attention to the visual periphery matters for both hearing and deaf signers, but only deaf signers show enhanced processing of visual attention in the periphery. Compared with non-language stimuli that also involve organized, sequenced hand movements, signed sentences activate posterior superior temporal cortex, which is Wernicke's area.","children":["Signed languages therefore have linguistic form, grammar and language-related neural organization."]},{"text":"A coherent account has to separate shared language organization from **sensory adaptations**. Neither the view that sign is just gesture nor the view that modality makes no difference captures this evidence, and the findings jointly show that linguistic experience and sensory history contribute in different ways.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p043":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport** asked which of two versions of the **critical period** hypothesis their data fit better. The **exercise hypothesis** says that early language use preserves language-learning capacity, while the **maturational-state hypothesis** allows capacity to decline despite early use.","children":[]},{"text":"Both versions assume an early advantage, but they differ in what early successful experience protects. Later second-language difficulty in people who already acquired a first language is more directly compatible with maturation than with complete preservation through exercise.","children":["A late learner has already exercised language capacity, so the difficulty that late learners show favors the maturational-state version."]},{"text":"Does this change the interpretation of the first-language data? It does not make early first-language experience unimportant. Early access and age-related change address related but distinct questions, and the exercise account allows preservation through use while the maturational-state account predicts decline with maturation itself.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p044":[{"text":"**Mayberry and colleagues** compared later language learning after different early language histories. Many deaf children do not get sign language input but have an otherwise typical upbringing.","children":[]},{"text":"Children who first encountered American Sign Language between the ages of nine and fifteen had real difficulty learning it when it was their first language. A comparison group that went deaf after first learning a spoken language did much better, and these differences persisted into adulthood.","children":["Deaf individuals without accessible early language performed worse than those who had acquired an early spoken or signed language."]},{"text":"Age of starting ASL is not the same as age of first language access. The comparison separates late first-language learning from late acquisition of an additional language, and earlier linguistic experience can support learning a new language later.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p045":[{"text":"A possible confound is whether the **blocking effect** arises at the level of **visual object recognition**. Two responses address this question. First, the objects were **normed**, which means rated so that objects in the same category were not more visually similar to one another than objects in different categories on average.","children":[]},{"text":"Second, a control experiment blocked visual similarity along, and the blocking effect was observed in the first block of trials. The visual manipulation therefore affected early trials, while the semantic pattern of interest developed differently across repetitions.","children":["So the effects in the first experiment are unlikely to be due to visual similarity."]},{"text":"A control is informative when it tests the alternative mechanism directly. Matching appearance and manipulating visual similarity provide complementary evidence, and the difference in time course helps tell visual recognition apart from the proposed lexical contribution.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p046":[{"text":"**Hermens and colleagues** found no effect when people saw several objects at the same time, so perhaps something is different about seeing objects in serial. **Belke and colleagues** addressed this by having participants view displays of four items at once while producing their names in serial order.","children":[]},{"text":"Showing all the objects at once reduces dependence on a succession of isolated pictures, but serial naming still requires selecting and producing one label after another. This separates display timing from naming order, which helps locate the source of interference.","children":["Simultaneous visibility does not remove the need for sequential lexical selection. **Eye tracking** adds timing information about how each object was processed."]}],"m3-review-p047":[{"text":"Participants' eye movements were measured while they named the objects. **Gaze duration** records how long the eyes stay on an object, and **eye-speech lag** is the interval between leaving an object and beginning its spoken name.","children":[]},{"text":"A participant can move the eyes toward the next object before saying the current one, so these two measures capture overlapping parts of preparation. Different blocking effects on the two measures can help constrain which processing stage is affected.","children":["One total naming time can hide several processes. Coordinating eye and speech measures reveals where a condition begins to change performance, because looking at an object and speaking its name are not the same event."]}],"m3-review-p048":[{"text":"**Semantic blocking** increases gaze duration only after the first presentation cycle. Overall speech duration decreases over the course of the experiment, but it shows no blocking effect.","children":[]},{"text":"There is no effect on **eye-speech lag**. The blocking effect numerically seems to go in the opposite direction, but this is not significant, so it should not be reported as evidence for a reversed effect.","children":[]},{"text":"Gaze duration is linked to **lemma selection**, while the lag is linked to **phonological encoding**. The location of the effect across measures matters more than a general statement that naming is slower, because the time course and selectivity constrain the proposed mechanism.","children":["The gaze pattern supports a contribution at lemma selection rather than at articulation."]}],"m3-review-p049":[{"text":"The question is whether phoneme identification for word recognition works more like **thresholded** processing, labeled lumber mill, or **cascaded** processing, labeled food web. **McMurray and colleagues** tested identification of a /b/ and /p/ continuum in a visual world display, with eye movements recorded.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants heard the continuum while viewing pictures including BEAR and PEAR. **Voice onset time**, or VOT, is the delay between releasing the consonant and the start of voicing, and it can change how strongly the sound supports /b/ or /p/.","children":["A thresholded account would pass a completed categorical decision onward."]},{"text":"A **cascaded** account allows graded information to influence word candidates before uncertainty is fully resolved. The critical test is whether within-category differences influence lexical responses, because a binary final choice alone may hide that information. The display includes a target, a phonetic competitor and unrelated fillers, and looking at the competitor is informative because its name overlaps with the heard word.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p050":[{"text":"Participants used the mouse to select the image most consistent with what they heard, such as BEAR or PEAR, and their choices produced a typical step-like **identification function**. Shorter VOTs favored the /b/ response and longer VOTs favored the /p/ response.","children":[]},{"text":"Near the boundary, the preferred category changes rapidly, so the steep transition marks where the choice flips.","children":["A forced choice can end in one category even when processing used graded evidence, which is why eye movements provide a separate measure."]}],"m3-review-p051":[{"text":"Eye movements reveal that ambiguity is **gradient**. Looks to the competitor are higher for sounds close to the boundary between /b/ and /p/, so there are more looks to the competitor close to the boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"A participant can ultimately select BEAR while spending more time looking at PEAR when the sound is close to the boundary. This supports the claim that fine phonetic information stays relevant to lexical activation, instead of disappearing after a categorical phoneme decision.","children":["Final identification and moment-by-moment competition reveal different aspects of processing, so a sharp choice boundary can coexist with graded lexical uncertainty."]},{"text":"The gaze curves group trials by VOT and final response, and the effect concerns looks to the unchosen competitor, not merely how often participants click the wrong image.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p052":[{"text":"**Competitor fixation** increases as a token approaches the category boundary from either side. For BEAR responses, a more /p/-like VOT increases looks to PEAR, and for PEAR responses, a more /b/-like VOT increases looks to BEAR.","children":[]},{"text":"The two line types separate early and late observation windows, so they distinguish time windows rather than two groups of participants. The gradient remains visible in the later competition measure, which supports continued sensitivity to within-category phonetic detail.","children":["The result favors **cascaded** use of graded evidence over a model that passes on only a completed phoneme label."]},{"text":"The relevant predictor is the **distance from the boundary**, not only which side of the boundary a token occupies, so similar final choices can still have different lexical-competition profiles.","children":[]}]}};
